# Linux Boot Process & Core System Components – Complete Reference

## 1. Boot Sequence Overview
UEFI Firmware → GRUB2 → Kernel → initramfs → systemd (PID 1) → Target/Runlevel

---

## 2. UEFI Firmware
- Virtual (UTM/VMware) or physical (Mac/PC hardware)
- Loads bootloader from EFI System Partition (`/boot/efi`)
- Not part of Linux OS; provided by platform
- Key Paths:
  - `/boot/efi/EFI/ubuntu/grubx64.efi` – Ubuntu GRUB binary
  - `/boot/efi/EFI/BOOT/bootx64.efi` – Fallback bootloader

---

## 3. GRUB2 Bootloader
- Loaded by UEFI from ESP
- Presents boot menu, loads kernel + initrd
- Config source: `/etc/default/grub`
- Generated config: `/boot/grub/grub.cfg` (**never edit directly**)

### Essential Commands
sudo nano /etc/default/grub   # Edit settings
sudo update-grub              # Regenerate grub.cfg

### Manual Boot (Emergency)
set root=(hdX,gptY)
linux /boot/vmlinuz-VER root=/dev/XXX ro
initrd /boot/initrd.img-VER
boot

### Key Kernel Parameters
| Parameter       | Purpose                     |
|-----------------|-----------------------------|
| root=           | Specify root partition      |
| ro / rw         | Read-only / read-write root |
| init=/bin/bash  | Emergency shell             |
| nomodeset       | Disable GPU drivers         |
| quiet           | Suppress boot messages      |
| systemd.unit=   | Override default target     |

---

## 4. Linux Kernel
- Core OS component managing hardware, memory, processes
- Binary file: `/boot/vmlinuz-*`
- Modules: `/lib/modules/<version>/`
- Never edit kernel files manually
- Install: `sudo apt install linux-image-*`
- Remove old: `sudo apt autoremove`

### Essential Commands
uname -r                    # Running kernel version
lsmod                       # List loaded modules
modprobe <module>           # Load module
rmmod <module>              # Unload module
dmesg | tail                # Recent kernel messages
journalctl -k               # Kernel log via journald

---

## 5. initramfs (Initial RAM Filesystem)
- Temporary root in RAM to load storage/filesystem drivers
- File: `/boot/initrd.img-*` (must match kernel version)
- Discarded after real root is mounted
- Config Dir: `/etc/initramfs-tools/`
- Add modules: `/etc/initramfs-tools/modules`
- Main config: `initramfs.conf`

### Essential Commands
sudo update-initramfs -u          # Regenerate for current kernel
lsinitramfs /boot/initrd.img-VER  # Inspect contents
cat /etc/initramfs-tools/modules  # View forced modules

---

## 6. systemd Targets (Modern Runlevels)
| Target              | Legacy Runlevel | Use Case               |
|---------------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| rescue.target       | 1/S             | Single-user maintenance|
| multi-user.target   | 3               | CLI server             |
| graphical.target    | 5               | Desktop with GUI       |
| poweroff.target     | 0               | Shutdown               |
| reboot.target       | 6               | Reboot                 |

### Essential Commands
systemctl get-default                     # Current default target
systemctl set-default multi-user.target   # Set CLI as default
systemctl set-default graphical.target    # Set GUI as default
systemctl isolate rescue.target           # Switch to rescue now
systemctl isolate multi-user.target       # Switch to CLI now
systemctl isolate graphical.target        # Switch to GUI now

---

## 7. Filesystem Hierarchy (/boot Focus)
| Path                      | Purpose                          |
|---------------------------|----------------------------------|
| /boot/vmlinuz-*           | Compressed kernel image          |
| /boot/initrd.img-*        | Initial RAM filesystem           |
| /boot/config-*            | Kernel build configuration       |
| /boot/System.map-*        | Kernel symbol table              |
| /boot/grub/               | GRUB2 config & modules           |
| /boot/efi/                | EFI System Partition mount point |
| /lib/modules/<ver>/       | Kernel modules directory         |
| /etc/default/grub         | GRUB user configuration          |
| /etc/initramfs-tools/     | initramfs configuration          |

---

## 8. Critical Rules
1. Never edit `/boot/grub/grub.cfg` → use `/etc/default/grub` + `update-grub`
2. Never modify kernel/initrd binaries directly
3. Always regenerate initramfs after module/config changes
4. Kernel version must match initrd and modules directory
5. `/boot/efi` contains UEFI binaries; `/boot` contains kernel/initrd
6. Use package manager for kernel installs/removals

